The Bitbybit OCCT Kernel (built on OpenCASCADE Technology):

Each build of the Bitbybit OCCT Kernel (bitbybit-dev-occt*.wasm) is one
WebAssembly file in which OpenCASCADE Technology (LGPL-2.1 with the Open
CASCADE exception), Draco (Apache 2.0), RapidJSON (MIT) and, in the
multithreaded build, mimalloc (MIT) are statically linked with the Bitbybit
kernel layer, whose source is not published. The kernel is licensed under the
MIT License: free to use, modify and redistribute in any project, personal or
commercial, though closed source. Its license and its own NOTICE ship in the
@bitbybit-dev/occt npm package (LICENSE, NOTICE) and beside the kernel in
wasm/ (bitbybit-dev-occt.LICENSE.txt, bitbybit-dev-occt.NOTICE.txt, with
the license texts of its open-source components in wasm/licenses/).

Relinking with a modified OCCT: because OCCT and the kernel layer are
linked into one file, using a modified OCCT means relinking that file. Each
build's relinking kit holds the materials to do so (the kernel layer as
compiled object files, the Draco build, the script that builds OCCT for
WebAssembly, the link command and the toolchain version) and is published
free of charge as the release relink-kit-<kernel file name without .wasm>
of https://github.com/bitbybit-dev/bitbybit-assets/releases. For a build
whose kit is not there, write to info@bitbybit.dev; this offer is valid for
at least three years after we last distribute that build. Each kit names the
exact OCCT commit its build was compiled from.

A relinked kernel works with the JavaScript glue its own link produces, and
the Runner's OCCT worker bundles the glue of the original kernel: to run a
relinked kernel, use a worker that works with your glue - the Runner's own
worker, modified for your own use as the Bitbybit Runner License allows, or a
worker built from the MIT @bitbybit-dev/occt-worker package (without the
Runner's advanced OCCT functions, such as 3D text) - put the worker and the
kernel under the original file names in your self-hosted copy of the
Bitbybit CDN assets, and point the Runner's cdnUrl option at that copy.

